### Expected Behavior


Returning `redirectURL` from `session.webRequest.onBeforeRequest` for a CORS
preflight should either redirect the request, reject the redirect, or fail the
request safely. It must not crash the Electron main process.



### Actual Behavior

Redirecting an `OPTIONS` CORS preflight deterministically crashes the main
process with an access violation.

Stock `electron@43.0.0` from npm produced:

```text
Exception code: 0xc0000005
Fault offset: 0x00000000040f22fd
```

The same reproduction using Castlabs Electron `v43.0.0+wvcus` produced:

```text
Exception code: 0xc0000005
Fault offset: 0x0000000004172b2d
```

Symbolizing that build with its exact private PDB produced:

```text
electron!network::mojom::URLLoaderClientProxy::OnReceiveRedirect+0x12d
electron!electron::ProxyingURLLoaderFactory::InProgressRequest::ContinueToBeforeRedirect+0xcc
electron!electron::ProxyingURLLoaderFactory::InProgressRequest::HandleBeforeRequestRedirect+0x32e
```

At the crash:

```text
URLLoaderClientProxy *this = null
for_cors_preflight_ = 1
current_request_uses_header_client_ = 1
```

This indicates that the special preflight `InProgressRequest` has no target
URL-loader client, but the header-client redirect path reaches
`ContinueToBeforeRedirect`, which calls
`target_client_->OnReceiveRedirect(...)`.

The relevant source is:

https://github.com/electron/electron/blob/v43.0.0/shell/browser/net/proxying_url_loader_factory.cc

### Testcase

`package.json`:

```json
{
  "name": "electron-preflight-redirect-repro",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "private": true,
  "main": "main.cjs",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "electron ."
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "electron": "43.0.0"
  }
}
```

`main.cjs`:

```javascript
const {app, BrowserWindow, session} = require("electron");
const http = require("node:http");
const path = require("node:path");

app.setPath("userData", path.join(__dirname, "profile"));

function listen(server) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        server.once("error", reject);
        server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", () => resolve(server.address().port));
    });
}

app.whenReady().then(async () => {
    const target = http.createServer((_request, response) => {
        response.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", "*");
        response.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Headers", "x-trigger");
        response.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Methods", "POST");
        response.end("ok");
    });
    const targetPort = await listen(target);

    const source = http.createServer((_request, response) => {
        response.setHeader("Content-Type", "text/html");
        response.end(`<script>
            fetch("http://127.0.0.1:${targetPort}/data", {
                method: "POST",
                headers: {"X-Trigger": "1"},
                body: "trigger"
            }).catch(console.error);
        </script>`);
    });
    const sourcePort = await listen(source);

    session.defaultSession.webRequest.onBeforeRequest(
        {urls: ["http://127.0.0.1/*"]},
        (details, callback) => {
            if (details.method === "OPTIONS" && details.url.endsWith("/data")) {
                callback({
                    redirectURL: `http://127.0.0.1:${targetPort}/noop`,
                });
                return;
            }
            callback({});
        },
    );

    const window = new BrowserWindow({show: false});
    await window.loadURL(`http://127.0.0.1:${sourcePort}/`);

    setTimeout(() => {
        console.log("SURVIVED_10_SECONDS");
        source.close();
        target.close();
        app.quit();
    }, 10_000);
});
```

Run:

```sh
npm install
npm start
```

The Electron main process crashes before `SURVIVED_10_SECONDS` is printed. Both
servers bind only to `127.0.0.1`; no external website or extension is involved.



### Testcase Gist URL

_No response_

### Additional Information

The application-side workaround is to convert `onBeforeRequest` redirects for
the `OPTIONS` method into `{cancel: true}`. Other request methods can continue
redirecting normally.

A possible Electron-side fix would be to mirror the existing preflight guards
used by the other redirect paths before calling
`HandleBeforeRequestRedirect`, or otherwise fail the request safely when no
target client exists.
